WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:08.241
♪ ♪

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:11.811
BERRY: You have, in fact,
after emancipation,

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.748
the rise of vigilante groups,

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:16.783
the Ku Klux Klan,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.785
the Knights of
the White Camelias.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:21.454
They had different
names and different places,

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:25.725
and they saw it as
their job to intimidate and

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:27.694
keep Black people
in their place.

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:34.334
It's much like, today we talk
about the "Karens," you know,

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:37.570
who have felt called upon when
they see some Black person

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:40.540
doing some routine thing that
they think is out of the way,

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:42.175
or they shouldn't be doing it.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:47.247
That they should take
it upon themselves to
impose a kind of order,

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:50.517
in addition to what ordinary
law enforcement people,

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.485
who are official, were doing.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:08.234
BERRY: And Emmett Till was
the most notorious example.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:19.646
ALLWOOD: Emmett Till was
a young boy from Chicago.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:22.482
He was 14 years-old
and as the story goes,

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.452
he whistled at a White woman.

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:30.557
And that night,
these White rednecks,

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.492
they murdered him.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:40.266
And his mother decided to have
an open casket so everybody

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.469
could view what
happened to her son.

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:47.373
But that's the way
it was in those days.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.509
If you even looked at a
White woman in the South,

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.845
you could lose your life.

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:57.517
That was the first time I
had firsthand knowledge and

00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:59.752
experience with
the Jim Crow South.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.190
The Ku Klux Klan,
the lynchings.

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:09.295
Just try to imagine
what it was like living
under those conditions.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.600
It just made you angry.

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:20.607
BERRY: This showed you the
force of the vigilante as uh,

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.809
a reinforcement of the law.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:29.148
And with Ahmaud Arbery, the
same kind of thing happened.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.419
WOMAN: I know,
that's fine, that's fine.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:37.757
BERRY: And this kind of
behavior is no different from

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:42.262
what happened to
Black people earlier in
all kinds of situations.

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:48.768
And so you would think that
the law would be on your side.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:52.705
And the effort of the
Civil Rights Movement

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.742
was to try to get the
law to be on our side,

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.178
to fulfill the promises,
as they put it, of the

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.714
Declaration of Independence
and of the Constitution.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:03.783
The language that was there,

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.386
but for everybody and
not just for some people.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:10.523
But of course, in '63,
we had Birmingham.

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:15.762
♪ ♪

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:18.464
&lt;i&gt;♪ Oh ♪&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:20.333
♪ When the river ♪♪

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.637
BERRY: Dogs biting people,
women and children.

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.274
Not college students,
but young children,

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:30.577
10 years-old,
9 years-old, 8 years-old,

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:33.246
who were out there protesting.

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:36.115
It was night after night.

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:39.385
Vivid, graphic images
which were undeniable.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:47.327
There were also, police had
long used a tactic of when

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:51.598
they grabbed somebody that
they wanted to stop or harass,

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:55.368
they would push them down and
then they would put their knee

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:58.271
on the person's neck to
hold them down, you see,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.741
to make them
submit to their power.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.815
And these very brutal images,

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:11.451
which the
President couldn't deny,

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.519
because no one
else could deny it,

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:16.122
because you saw it
with your own eyes.

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:18.725
Made it possible for the
movement to go forward.

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:25.765
And so the civil rights
leaders decided that we should

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:27.634
have a march on Washington.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.203
♪ It's knocking at my door ♪♪

